WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.750
Beauty And The Beast director Bill Condon
did a great job adapting Disney's classic

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.200
animation into a live-action movie, but during
the editing process some intriguing moments

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.480
got cut including some funny battle scenes
in the castle and a very different take on

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.080
the song "Days In The Sun".

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.820
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and with the Blu-ray of Beauty And The Beast

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.970
out, I'm going to break down all the official
deleted scenes and discuss the many easter

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.860
eggs and call-backs to the original animated
movie as well as other details we never got

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.590
to see in the theatrical release.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.630
I'm also starting a new giveaway on this video.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.730
For a chance to win this gorgeous hardback
book "Tale as Old as Time: The Art And Making

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.050
of Beauty And The Beast", just subscribe,
leave a comment about the movie, and make

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.429
sure you also answer the question I'll be
asking later in the video.

00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:47.710
By the way, obviously there are spoilers ahead
if you haven't seen the movie!

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.489
During the battle between the enchanted objects
and the villagers at the Beast's castle, there's

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:54.500
a funny little moment that was cut with the
Village Lasses.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.170
The scene shows the Lasses, armed with pitchforks,
chasing Chip and FrouFrou, through the castle

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.199
and into the kitchen.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:04.860
Which is where Cuisinier, the castle's chef-turned-stove,
covers them in treacle and feathers, and they

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.090
run off screaming.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.530
This scene is actually a lovely call-back
to the original animated movie where LeFou

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:15.710
and two other men with assorted weaponry race
after the castle's enchanted footstool, who's

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.620
stolen one of LeFou's shoes, until they're
threatened away by the kitchen's fiery stove.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.159
Also deleted from the battle at the castle
is a scene where Lumiere is attacked by one

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:29.710
of the female villagers, and Cogsworth comes
to his friend's rescue, sliding down the bannister,

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.799
and shooting the villager in the butt.

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:36.200
However, when she turns round, Cogsworth is
shocked to find that the person he's shot

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.200
is Clothilde.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.820
Now, if you remember, at the end of the film
when the enchanted objects turn back into

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.250
human servants, we discover that Clothilde
is actually Cogsworth's wife, and that he's

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.200
definitely not happy to be reunited with her!

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.070
Which explains why, in this deleted scene,
Cogsworth continues to fight her off with

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.810
a sword even after he knows who she is!

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.530
Again, this deleted scene is a delightful
throwback to the original animation, where

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.100
Cogsworth saves Lumiere from being melted
by LeFou.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.510
In that animated scene, Cogsworth choses between
a pistol and a pair of scissors, and comes

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.280
barrelling down the bannister, stabbing LeFou
in the butt with the scissors, using them

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.160
like a sword.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.780
In the final live-action film, Plumette strikes
LeFou in the face with her feathers, but we

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.610
don't see what happens to her next.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.750
The section that was cut from the movie after
that shows LeFou rushing after Plumette and

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.180
catching her in a net.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.400
However, Lumiere comes to her rescue when
he uses his candles to burn LeFou's butt.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.459
In the animated classic, it's not LeFou but
one of the other villagers who's mistreating

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:38.660
the enchanted duster by plucking out her feathers,
but again, Lumiere saves the day by torching

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.099
the villager's behind!

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:45.440
During the mob scene at the castle, LeFou
continues to question his allegiance to Gaston,

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.450
and in a humorous deleted scene, he hides
away from the fighting in one of the castle's

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:50.450
many rooms.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.739
Unfortunately for LeFou, the room he picks
is an enchanted bathroom where a new character

00:02:54.739 --> 00:03:00.780
called Monsieur Toilette drenches him with
water, which sends LeFou rushing off screaming.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.640
Then at the end of the film, when the household
objects transform back into human beings,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.739
there's another deleted scene where actor
Stephen Merchant appears playing Monsieur

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:14.260
Toilette just standing there, clothes wet
and dirty, mouth agape, basically completely

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.129
horrified by what he's been through during
the curse.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:22.069
When LeFou arrives he identifies Monsieur
Toilette due to the terrible odour, and asks

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:23.709
him what he's going to do now.

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:26.569
To which Toilette replies: brush my teeth!

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:29.950
And if you're wondering why all these little
moments I've been talking about so far were

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:35.739
cut from the end movie, it was really a question
of timing and taste, as according to director

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:39.840
Bill Condon the movie was running long and
some of the humour was considered a bit low-brow

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.860
in comparison to the rest of the film.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.629
There are some lyrics missing from the Mob
Song in the live-action film compared to the

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:47.870
animated movie.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:52.280
The missing section would have fit into the
film just after Gaston consults the Beast's

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.319
magic mirror to check where the castle is,
and it features the villagers making their

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:00.080
way through the woods, while singing the lines:
"We don't like what we don't understand.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.290
In fact, it scares us.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.790
And this monster is mysterious at least.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.379
Bring your guns bring your knives.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.170
Take your children and your wives.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.860
We'll save our village and our lives.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:11.180
We'll kill the beast."

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.329
Then straight after those words, the deleted
scene shows the villagers breaking down the

00:04:15.329 --> 00:04:17.500
ice gates that lead up to the castle.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.320
This shot of the villagers storming through
the broken gates is beautiful and kind of

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.110
symbolic as it looks like they're walking
through the jaws of a beast.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.310
I can see why Bill Condon was sad to cut the
scene, though he did so as he was aware of

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.970
the film's running time and also felt it downplayed
the tension of the moment.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.190
A scene that was cut from the movie's opening
section shows Belle giving the beggar woman

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.669
Agathe some bread and jam, which she's very
grateful for.

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:45.639
It's just a small moment between these two
characters, who we don't see interact during

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.460
the final film, and it highlights Belle's
kind and compassionate nature, and also emphasises

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.060
how different she is from the other villagers
who treat Agathe unkindly like an outsider.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.650
And, on top of that, this deleted scene may
also go some way to answering the question

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:03.460
of what happened to the baguette that Belle
bought from the baker, which was in her dress

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:08.250
pocket during the opening song one minute
but mysteriously disappeared the next!

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.810
It would have been nice to see a bit more
of Agathe in the movie because her character

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:15.940
is rather important, after all, she is the
Enchantress in disguise.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:19.270
And if you'd like to learn all about the secrets
of the Enchantress in this movie, then check

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:23.440
out my video on that very subject by tapping
in the top right of the screen.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.090
After the townspeople throw Belle's soaking
wet clothes all over the ground because they

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.139
don't like her new laundry cleaning invention,
there's a scene that was cut from the film

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:36.240
which shows Gaston arriving, blasting his
gun for the crowd to make way and telling

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.840
them they're behaving badly and should go
home.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.960
While this may seem, on the surface, like
a decent thing to do, as usual, Gaston has

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.039
his own best interests at heart, and those
interests involve convincing Belle to marry

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:51.130
him so that he can have what he believes he
deserves: the best wife in town.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:56.039
It's always fun watching Luke Evans play the
incredibly arrogant but ultimately clueless

00:05:56.039 --> 00:06:00.389
Gaston, and I love the line he says to LeFou
after he tells the crowd to leave,

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:04.250
"I'm not sure what's going on here, but I'm
pretty sure I just fixed it";

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:09.460
however, I can see why this scene was cut
as it doesn't add anything new or revelatory

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.020
to what we learn at the start of the movie
about the characters of Belle and Gaston and

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.130
their attitude and feelings towards one another.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.100
The musical number "Days In The Sun" was a
new song introduced in the live-action movie,

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:24.130
however, there is an alternate version of
the song which begins with the Beast dreaming

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.759
of his mother singing to him when he was lying
in bed as a young boy.

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:31.691
In this alternate version, the prince's mother
sings the first part of the song, saying that

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:34.490
she'll never leave him until her own life
is done.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.389
Now, the middle of this deleted version is
the same as in the final film, but at the

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:43.270
end of the cut version, the prince's mother
has died in her bed and her young son watches

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.289
over her until he's pulled away by his father.

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:50.800
This original version had to be partly reshot
after it was discovered that test audiences

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.270
were mixing up actress Harriet Jones, who
plays the prince's mother, with Hattie Morahan

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.390
who plays Agathe, aka the Enchantress.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:03.070
Because of this confusion, the scene was changed
with Harriet Jones's role cut back to a non-singing

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.660
part where she just lay dead in her bed while
the prince looked on.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:11.259
And in the re-filmed scene, she also had dark,
rather than blond, hair to further differentiate

00:07:11.259 --> 00:07:13.180
her from the character of the Enchantress.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:17.600
Also, the young prince's part was recast with
a young actor who could sing as he now had

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.830
to sing the first few lines of the song as
it appears the final film.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.650
It's a shame the original version wasn't used
as actress Harriet Jones has such a lovely

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:30.590
singing voice and that version packs an even
stronger emotional punch because the prince's

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:34.790
mother sings to him that she'll never leave
him, but by the end of the song, she's dead

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.970
and he's asking why she left him.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:41.250
Plus, the alternate version gives at least
one real moment on screen for the prince's

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:45.849
mother, whereas her time on screen in the
final film was reduced to almost nothing.

00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:49.759
I feel like instead of altering this scene,
they could have simply re-filmed it as it

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:54.080
was but changing the colour of the mother's
hair to make the difference from the Enchantress

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.080
even clearer.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.389
So, what do you think about the deleted scenes
and where there any you think should have

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:00.389
been in the movie?

